  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 158 AM MDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nThere are weird things that hit you when working at night, \nespecially on a lack of sleep (that is me tonight). I just had a \nmemory from when I was 12 years old back in 1983. The memory is the \nfirst \"R\" rated movie I was able to see. And that movie was the \nclassic comedy \"48 Hours\" starring Nick Nolte and Eddie Murphy.\nAnd you may be asking, how does this relate to the weather? Well, \nthe premise of the movie was the Eddie Murphy's character was on a \n48 hour pass from prison to help Nick Nolte catch a criminal. \nAnd 48 hours is about the time a relief we will have from the \nrelentless heat over much of the past month. The past couple of \ndays have been rather nice, despite the smoke. However, like \nMurphy's pass came to an end, our relief from the heat will \ncome to an end starting today but especially later in the week.\n\nThe reason is the ridge that had retreated southward somewhat after \nthe passage of the cold front Sunday night and Monday will begin \nbuild back northward today and bring another period of above normal \ntemperatures starting today in southern Wyoming and building across \nmuch of the area into the weekend. It seems to be following a \npattern, much like the past several weeks, with the warmest high \nfrom Friday into the weekend. The 700 millibar temperatures rise to \nplus 17 today across the south, rising as high as plus 21 by the \nweekend. Most guidance is consistent in keeping the warmest \ntemperatures across central and southern Wyoming, with somewhat \ncooler temperatures aloft over northern Wyoming. It will still be \nabove normal even there though. At this point, it does not look \nquite as hot as the past few weekends though. We will be entering \nsolar autumn tomorrow, so the sun won't be quite as strong. Nights \nare also getting noticeably longer, allowing more time to cool \noff and less time to warm up during the day. And, smoke from the\nwildfires over the Pacific northwest and now in western Wyoming\nmay keep temperatures a few degrees cooler. Nevertheless, from \nFriday through Sunday, locations in the Wind River and the \nsouthern Big Horn Basin still have around a 1 in 2 chance of \nhigh temperatures over 100 in locations under 5000 feet in \nelevation.\n\nThe other concern will be potential critical fire weather. Humidity \nwill remain low thorough the period. The main variable will be wind. \nThe wind will remain rather gusty across southern Wyoming today. So, \nwe will hoist a Red Flag Warning for Sublette, Sweetwater and \nsouthern Lincoln Counties starting at noon today. We do have a bit \nof a conundrum for much of the rest of the workweek. The threat\nof critical fire weather may spread further to the north into \ncentral Wyoming. Humidity will remain below critical levels \nthrough the weekend. However, wind will be borderline for much \nof the week. With the uncertainty, we will take things one day \nat time. We will let the day crew reevaluate and see if warnings\nneed to be moved northward or extended. Right now, the chance \nis around 50 - 50.\n\nAs for precipitation, the chance is basically zero through Friday. A \nbit of moisture may try to move into western Wyoming on Saturday but \nit is of the mid level variety and may produce little or no rain and \npossibly dry lightning. Somewhat deeper moisture may return for \nSunday and into next week as the ridge again retreats to the south \nand some deeper monsoonal moisture may try to return to the area, \nbut trying to pick out a more active day is nearly impossible this \nfar out. With the ridge retreating to south, temperatures should \nalso cool off again, returning to more typical levels for early and \nmid August. However, the main story for the next several days will \nbe another return of the heat we have seen so frequently since the \naround the Fourth of July.",
